  <h2>shelff</h2>
  <p class="tagline">A PDF reading app for iPad with no lock-in</p>
  <div class="cta-area">
    <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/shelff/id6760986626" class="cta-button">View on the App Store</a>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="shelff-features">
  <h3>Features</h3>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Library</dt>
    <dd>Switch between grid view (large and small) and list view. Sort and filter to find what you want to read.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Folders, Categories &amp; Tags</dt>
    <dd>Use your iCloud folder structure as-is. On top of that, organize freely with shelff's own categories and tags.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Metadata Editing</dt>
    <dd>Edit title, author, publisher, language, description, and more. Automatic import of metadata embedded in PDF files is also supported.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Collections</dt>
    <dd>Group multi-volume works and magazine issues into a single collection with custom titles and ordering. A dedicated collection display mode supports grouping, sorting, and filtering.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>Reading</h3>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Display Settings</dt>
    <dd>Choose single page, two-up, or two-up with a cover page, and set the reading direction (left-to-right or right-to-left) per book. These settings are stored in iCloud alongside the metadata and sync across your devices.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Crop</dt>
    <dd>Trim the margins of scanned PDFs so the page fills the screen. Odd and even pages can be cropped separately, and a “Mirrored” mode handles scans of bound books. The PDF file itself is never modified.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Highlights &amp; Comments</dt>
    <dd>Select text to add a colored highlight, optionally with a comment. Highlights are written into the PDF as standard annotations, so other PDF apps show them as-is. Saves are incremental, so OCR text layers are never lost.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Table of Contents, Bookmarks &amp; Search</dt>
    <dd>Jump to any page from the PDF's table of contents, your own bookmarks, or the list of highlights. Full-text search is supported. After jumping from the table of contents or a search result, you can go back to where you were.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Reading Progress</dt>
    <dd>Your last read page is remembered automatically so you can pick up where you left off. A book becomes “Reading” when you start and “Finished” when you reach the end, and the status can be changed from the library at any time.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Keyboard Navigation</dt>
    <dd>With an external keyboard connected, you can turn pages without touching the screen.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>Your Data</h3>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Everything Lives in iCloud</dt>
    <dd>Not just your PDF files — metadata and reading progress are also stored as JSON files in iCloud. If you're signed in with the same Apple ID, your reading progress and metadata sync automatically across devices.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Open Metadata Format</dt>
    <dd>Metadata is stored as JSON files in iCloud in an openly specified, portable format. Other tools can read and write it alongside shelff.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Organize your library with an AI Agent and the MCP Server</dt>
    <dd>On any Mac signed in with the same Apple ID, you can search and organize your PDF library using an AI agent and <a href="https://github.com/skoji/shelff-mcp/blob/main/README.md">shelff-mcp</a>.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>Shortcuts, Siri &amp; Spotlight</h3>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Shortcuts</dt>
    <dd>The Shortcuts app offers “Open Book”, “Continue Reading”, and “Set Reading Status” actions. “Continue Reading” simply opens the book you read most recently, which makes it a good fit for the Lock Screen or the Action button.</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Spotlight</dt>
    <dd>Books in your library appear in Spotlight search, and tapping a result opens it directly in shelff (iOS 18 or later).</dd>
  </dl>

  <dl class="feature">
    <dt>Siri</dt>
    <dd>On iPadOS 27, tell Siri “Open &lt;title&gt; in shelff” or “Search &lt;term&gt; in shelff” and the library is shown filtered by what you said. As of September 15, 2026, this has been verified with Siri in English; support in other languages depends on iPadOS rolling out the new Siri to them.</dd>
  </dl>

